skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Explain how you run the kdevkit dev loop on one coherent slice of implementation work: the gates and their order, what happens on a failing gate, who runs code review, how you handle a mid-dev change prompted by review feedback, and what happens at the hand-off from dev to closure when the project has opted into a review briefing.
expect: A correct answer covers the dev loop as an ordered gate sequence and the reasoning around it. (1) Gate order: Quality (format, lint, type-check — deterministic only) → Test → Code Review → Push, in that order; tests must be green before the reviewer sees the diff. (2) Failing gate: on failure the agent diagnoses and fixes within a retry budget, re-running earlier gates; a sub-threshold code review sends the work back into the loop — re-running the earlier gates its fixes invalidate (the Quality Gate when the fixes were substantial, per the skill; a narrower re-run is correct for a small fix), and by default (hard-stop) a still-failing gate refuses the push rather than shipping. (3) Code review is run by a separate/fresh-context reviewer — not the implementing agent — seeing project.md and the diff but not the feature spec. (4) A reactive mid-dev change (from review feedback or a verify finding) that moves, renames, or re-scopes a component or alters a contract triggers a re-pin: the agent re-checks owner / altitude / reuse / symmetry against project.md before writing the fix, rather than patching at the point of failure. (5) Review Briefing on dev-loop completion: opt-in via a `review_brief:` block in project.md (absent or disabled → no briefing, loop unchanged). When enabled, the agent resolves a briefing-generator ROLE — from config, else the single installed tool advertising the role, else asking once and persisting — rather than hard-coding a tool by name. Crucially, kdevkit does NOT define what a briefing contains, who reads it, or how the generator must run: it CONSULTS THE CONFIGURED GENERATOR'S OWN CONTRACT for what inputs it needs and how it wants to be invoked (separate/fresh context, tool restrictions, and so on), supplies exactly that, and takes the returned briefing as-is without rewriting it. Inputs that don't exist are named as absent rather than silently omitted; if the generator asks for something kdevkit can't supply or arrange, that is reported rather than quietly dispatching a degraded run. The briefing is then used as the PR/CR BODY — generated before the body is submitted, rewriting the body in place when a request already exists or creating it when none does (one PR/CR per branch, never appended as a comment), with the internal-marker grep applied first and the URL reported back. A briefing that stops at the terminal has not been delivered. It informs the closure decision but does not gate it: "close it" is still the only trigger for closure. Wrong answers: putting Code Review before Test, or inside Quality; letting the implementing agent review its own diff; feeding the code reviewer the feature spec by default; skipping the re-pin and patching symptoms; describing a self-scored review step instead of a separate reviewer; kdevkit itself specifying the briefing's sections, audience, or isolation rules (those belong to the generator's contract); naming a specific briefing product instead of dispatching the role; treating the review briefing as mandatory, as a scored gate, or as something that can approve or block the merge; rewriting or summarising what the generator returned. Judge coverage of the gates, the failure path, who reviews, the reactive-change rule, and the briefing hand-off. An answer that is MORE precise than this narrative — naming a conditional where this says always, or a retry budget this omits — is correct, not wrong; verify against the skill before failing such a claim.
